WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.010
- As long as human beings

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.050
have been lending things
to other human beings,

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.970
there's always been the question

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.302
of how likely is that person
that I'm lending that thing to

00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:11.400
going to give it back?

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.630
So there's always been this
question of credit worthiness.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.760
Is this person a good credit risk?

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.690
If I lend them money, how
likely are they going to be

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:25.010
to pay me back?

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.730
Now people have always found ways

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.160
to come up with a solution here.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.400
How much money do they have?

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.410
What kind of income do they have?

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.687
But in 1956, two gentlemen said,

00:00:35.687 --> 00:00:40.687
"Hey, I think we can apply
the ideas of mathematics,

00:00:40.812 --> 00:00:42.907
"the ideas of statistics,

00:00:42.907 --> 00:00:44.787
"to actually become a
little bit more scientific

00:00:44.787 --> 00:00:47.320
"about how credit worthy someone is."

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.030
and so these two gentlemen
started what is known

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.369
as the Fair Isaac Corporation.

00:00:54.369 --> 00:00:56.680
Fair Isaac Corporation.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.030
And when I first heard the name,

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.080
I assumed it was started by
a particularly fair Isaac,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.800
or at least an Isaac who
thought that he was fair,

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.870
so much so that he named it fair Isaac,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.780
but it's actually named
after the two founders,

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.513
Bill Fair, and Earl Isaac.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.570
Actually the first one
was Fair, comma, Isaac,

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:19.700
and Corporation, then changed
to Fair Isaac Corporation.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.743
And now it's often known as FICO.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.850
Often known as FICO.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.160
And what they came up with,

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.430
Bill Fair was an engineer,

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.410
Earl Isaac was a mathematician,

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:34.410
is an algorithm for computing
how credit worthy someone is.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.110
So they came up with an algorithm,

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.000
and the algorithm has changed since 1956,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.360
because clearly, technology has changed,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.960
and our understanding of statistics,

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.080
and even the world itself

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.610
that we're trying to
understand has changed.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.485
But they started focusing
on the problem of,

00:01:49.485 --> 00:01:52.115
given some inputs, can I give a score

00:01:52.115 --> 00:01:56.370
that can give you a sense of
someone's credit worthiness.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:57.662
So they would take inputs,

00:01:57.662 --> 00:02:02.540
they would take inputs like
total debt, total debt,

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.930
and actually not just total
debt, but available debt.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.110
Total debt to how much credit

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:07.950
someone could get access to.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.930
Things like payment history,

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.900
so have they paid all their debts on time?

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.290
Payment history.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:19.090
And other things, like
length of credit history,

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.700
and the types of credit.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.700
So length, length, and types of credit.

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:30.080
And other things like how many people

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.080
are inquiring about this person's credit,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:32.940
and things like that.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.780
And then they came up with a score.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.010
And today, the modern FICO score,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.150
and the FICO really is the, I would say,

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.790
the dominant type of credit score.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:43.140
There are other types of algorithms

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:43.973
that people use,

00:02:43.973 --> 00:02:46.070
but FICO is still the predominant one.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.573
It will give you a score
that is between 300 and 850.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.310
So 300 at the low end,
850 at the high end.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.860
And the general view is, the general view,

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.660
and I actually talked to
some folks at some banks

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.601
to get their sense of things,

00:03:02.601 --> 00:03:07.601
that anything between
780 and 850 is excellent.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.600
You have excellent credit,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.872
you are amongst the best credit risk.

00:03:12.872 --> 00:03:14.870
You are viewed to be very likely

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.190
to pay off whatever loan
you're about to get.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:21.760
Anything between 720 and 780

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.170
is considered still above average.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.970
It's still considered very good.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:29.860
And anything between 650, 650.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.530
We'll do that a different color.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.050
Anything between 650 and 720

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.090
is considered kind of middle,
or average, or just okay.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.300
And anything less than 650 is not so good,

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.150
often referred to as sub-prime.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.180
Prime would be an excellent credit risk,

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.350
or a good credit risk,
or an okay credit risk.

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:51.360
Sub-prime means okay, this
person's a little bit riskier

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.780
to lend money to.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.460
So this is not so good.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:00.350
Now, the question is,
who actually collects

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:01.520
all of this information

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.520
in order to apply the FICO algorithm?

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.791
And this is where credit
bureaus come into the picture.

00:04:07.791 --> 00:04:12.020
So FICO, and it's a large
multi-billion dollar company now,

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.680
that does many, many, many things,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.350
but it's most famous for
devising the FICO algorithm.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.830
But the way that the credit
scores tend to be calculated

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:22.920
is by the credit bureaus.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.043
So the credit bureaus are the folks

00:04:25.043 --> 00:04:30.043
that will actually collect the data,

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:31.050
collect the data.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.510
Your total debt, payment
history, and other things,

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:36.160
apply the FICO algorithm,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.300
and then give you your
actual credit score.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.377
And the three major credit bureaus

00:04:41.377 --> 00:04:46.377
are TransUnion, TransUnion.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.750
Experian, Experian, and Equifax.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.990
So, if you were about
to lend someone money,

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.750
you could go to one of
these credit bureaus,

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.290
and you could do an inquiry
into their credit score.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.110
And actually, you,
yourself, as an individual

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.900
can go and inquire about your credit score

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.830
with each of these bureaus.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:10.100
And they offer different
types of algorithms,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.730
but the FICO one is the most used one.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:14.040
So you could get a FICO score

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.910
from each of these credit unions.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:17.350
And the score might be
a little bit different,

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.780
because they all might have
slightly different data here.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.350
But the way that it would generally work,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.700
is if you were to go to your bank

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:24.740
and ask for a mortgage,

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.600
or go to a credit card company,
and ask for a credit card,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.100
or get a car loan,

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:30.780
that entity, so let's say that

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.730
this is the bank right over here,

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.121
that might be interested
in lending you money,

00:05:35.121 --> 00:05:36.671
they are going to go,

00:05:36.671 --> 00:05:40.260
they are going to go to one
of these credit bureaus.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.090
They're gonna go to one
of these credit bureaus,

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.820
and say, "Hey, give me this
person's credit score."

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:45.720
And so that credit bureau's

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.787
gonna look at all the data
that it has on that person,

00:05:48.787 --> 00:05:50.970
put it into the FICO algorithm,

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.070
if the bank wants the FICO credit score,

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.120
and give a score right over here.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.580
And if you have excellent credit,

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:58.760
then you might get a low interest rate,

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.757
or you might definitely get the loan.

00:06:00.757 --> 00:06:04.740
If you have a good credit
score, you'll get the loan,

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.650
but it'll be a slightly
higher interest rate.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:07.760
But if you have really bad credit,

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.298
your probably not going
to get the loan at all.

00:06:10.298 --> 00:06:12.090
Now the last question
you're probably asking

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.577
is well, how do these credit
bureaus get the information?

00:06:15.577 --> 00:06:17.860
How do they know how
much total debt I have,

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:19.180
and my payment history,

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.150
and the lengths and types
of credit that I have,

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.280
and the number of inquiries that come in.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.630
Well, in exchange for lenders

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.390
being allowed to make these inquiries

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.270
and they usually pay the credit bureaus

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:31.580
to get the information,

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.010
they also agree to share this information.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:37.170
So let's say this bank
does give you the loan,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:38.623
they will tell the credit bureaus,

00:06:38.623 --> 00:06:41.081
they agree to tell the credit bureaus

00:06:41.081 --> 00:06:44.020
that they gave you that loan,

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:45.615
and they will also tell the credit bureau

00:06:45.615 --> 00:06:46.920
about your payment history.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.060
If you pay them all on time,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.560
the credit bureaus will know about that,

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.690
and they'll be able to
input into your FICO score.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:53.030
But if you don't pay it on time,

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.400
they will also know about that,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.643
and that will also affect your FICO score.

